-- ownership: a construction writes its base, so every OTHER operand —
-- items and later spreads alike — must not reach it (thesis §3.10,
-- Disjoint; T1's construction form). `xs ++ xs` (`selfAppend`) and
-- `[ …xs, 4, …xs ]` (`selfSpread`) read their base while extending it in
-- place: rejected, `disjoint`; so is a chain whose last link is its base
-- (`chainBack`, flattened into one construction), a base reached through a
-- second name (`renamed`), and a later spread that is a view of the base
-- (`spreadsView`). A later spread of a different list is only read
-- (`otherList`, accepted). Spreads' `twice` pins `[ …xs, …xs ]`; this
-- fixture adds the forms that put an item between the two spreads or reach
-- the base indirectly, so that leaving later spreads out of the disjoint
-- check fails here as well (the ownership oracle's adversarial review,
-- 2026-10-15: its Lean core has no append).
pub selfAppend : ⊤ → List Int
selfAppend ⊤ =
    xs = [ 1, 2, 3 ]
    xs ++ xs


pub selfSpread : ⊤ → List Int
selfSpread ⊤ =
    xs = [ 1, 2, 3 ]
    [ …xs, 4, …xs ]


pub chainBack : List Int, List Int → List Int
chainBack a b = a ++ b ++ a


pub renamed : List Int → List Int
renamed xs =
    ys = xs
    [ …xs, 0, …ys ]


pub spreadsView : List Int → List Int
spreadsView xs =
    case xs of
        [ _, …rest ] →
            xs ++ rest

        [] →
            []


pub otherList : List Int, List Int → List Int
otherList xs ys = [ …xs, 4, …ys ]
